
  Each of these gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle. We brainstorm the first one properly and the rest in
  turn.

  So the question that sizes everything: [1mis this actually multi-user?[0m It decides whether you need a server at all, and
  it is the single most expensive thing to change later.

  - [1mGenuinely multi-user[0m (several people, shared tasks, changes by one reach another): needs a backend, a database,
    accounts and auth, and a delivery channel. Notifications become a real system — an event stream, per-user
    subscriptions, read state, probably email or push. This is the version where your original request makes full sense,
    and it's a substantial build, not a weekend.
  - [1mSingle user, one browser[0m: no server, no accounts. "Notification" collapses into reminders — due dates, overdue
    items, an in-page toast. Cheap, fast, genuinely useful, and can run entirely from [38;5;153mindex.html[39m + local storage. But
    "users get notified when tasks they care about change" mostly evaporates, because the only person changing tasks is
    you.
  - [1mSingle user now, multi-user later[0m: build 1–2 solo, but keep the data model server-shaped so the jump isn't a
    rewrite. Costs some upfront discipline, buys optionality. This is what I'd recommend if you're unsure — the
    discipline is cheap now and a retrofit is not.

  My recommendation is the third unless you already know you have real collaborators, in which case go straight to the
  first and accept the size. The thing you cannot cheaply reverse is storage shape and identity; toast styling and
  delivery channels you can change any afternoon.
